// Conformance row K78 — the two DISCARD spellings of a singleton spawn form
// are REFUSED
//
// design 242 ruling 5: the result of `Thread.spawn { ... }` has a load-bearing
// fate, so no spelling may throw it away. The form hands back
// `Result<Thread<T>, SystemError>` (the OS may refuse a thread, SL-765), so the
// bare statement discards a `Result`, refused as every such discard is. That
// includes the EXPLICIT discard design 151 blessed for a `Result` — `let _ =`
// says "I read this and chose to drop it", and dropping a live thread handle
// is not a choice the language offers: drop elaboration refuses it, which a
// compile reaches only once the bare statement is gone (its own pin is
// `compiler/tests/drops/refuse/drop.must-consume.thread-let-underscore.saw`).
// The bare statement is the one a reader reaches for first (the
// fire-and-forget guess), and it used to compile into a sequential call plus
// thread overhead, because the handle's `deinit` joined.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: the result of this expression is `Result<std.task.Thread<Void>, std.systemerror.SystemError>` and is silently discarded

func work() {
    print("worked")
}

func main() {
    Thread.spawn { work() }
    let _ = Thread.spawn { work() }
    print("unreachable")
}
